<h1>Robotic Case Packing</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Robotic case packing uses delta (spider), articulated arm, or gantry robots to pick products from a conveyor and place them into corrugated cases. Vision systems locate products on the infeed belt, vacuum or mechanical grippers pick single or multiple items, and programmed motion paths place product into precise case patterns. Recipe-based changeover lets one cell run multiple product sizes and case configurations.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Delta, Articulated &amp; Gantry Robots</h2>
<h3>Delta (Spider) Robots</h3>
<p>Delta robots use three or four parallel-link arms attached to a fixed overhead frame. The lightweight arm structure allows very short pick-and-place cycles. ABB, for example, lists a 0.30 second cycle for its <a href="https://library.e.abb.com/public/a3759d3f3e7141deba89d1caeb1dd4e4/IRB%20360%20FlexPicker_Datasheet_US%20Letter.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRB 360-1/1130 FlexPicker</a> carrying 0.1 kg on a standard 25/305/25 mm test path. Real packing rates are lower and depend on product, gripper, and pattern. Delta robots pick from a moving conveyor belt using vision-guided tracking. The robot controller receives product position data from an upstream camera, calculates the pick trajectory, and synchronizes with the belt encoder to pick product in motion.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/robotic-jls-osprey-bp-case-loading.webp" width="1000" height="667" alt="An overhead delta robot with slender parallel arms and a multi-cup vacuum gripper lowers a bagged product into an open corrugated case beside a green infeed belt carrying more bags." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A JLS Osprey BP case packer loading bagged product. The overhead delta robot picks bags from the infeed belt with a vacuum cup gripper and places them into open cases on the conveyor alongside. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.jlsautomation.com/osprey-bp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">JLS Automation</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Delta robots suit light products: flow-wrapped snacks, pouches, bags, small cartons, and individual <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/cookie-cracker/">cookie packages</a>. Payload limits are low. ABB offers IRB 360 variants rated for 1, 3, 6, and 8 kg, and the rated payload must cover the gripper as well as the product.</p>
<h3>Articulated Arm Robots</h3>
<p>Articulated arm (6-axis) robots provide greater reach, payload, and flexibility than delta robots. Six joints allow the robot to approach the product from any angle, rotate it during placement, and reach into deep cases. Payload capacity spans a wide range across models, from small arms for single items to large arms that lift full layers. They cycle slower than delta robots because the heavier arm structure limits acceleration.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/robotic-brenton-rt1000-articulated-arms.webp" width="960" height="720" alt="Rendering of two yellow articulated arm robots inside a guarded cell, one placing product into an open corrugated case on a conveyor." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Rendering of the Brenton RT1000 top-load robotic case packer, with two articulated arm robots loading cases inside the guarded cell. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.brentonengineering.com/products/rt1000-robotic-case-packer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Brenton</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Articulated arms suit heavier products (cases, trays, multipacks), mixed-case packing (multiple SKUs into one case), and applications requiring product reorientation during placement. These robots also handle palletizing. A single robot can pack cases and palletize them in lower-volume applications.</p>
<h3>Gantry (Cartesian) Robots</h3>
<p>Gantry robots use linear axes (X, Y, Z) mounted on an overhead frame. The pick head travels along rails above the product conveyor and case conveyor. Gantry systems handle heavy loads and provide a large work envelope. They pick entire product layers (a complete row or partial case pattern) in a single motion, then place the layer into the case.</p>
<p>Gantry packers suit high-volume lines packing heavy products, beverage multipacks, canned goods trays, and bulk-packaged industrial products. Cycle rates are moderate, but each cycle places multiple products.</p>
