<h2 id="vision-heading">Vision Systems &amp; Conveyor Tracking</h2>
<p>Vision-guided robotic case packing uses cameras above the product infeed conveyor to locate, identify, and determine the orientation of each product. The vision system sends product coordinates and rotation angle to the robot controller, which calculates a pick trajectory synchronized to the belt's motion via encoder feedback.</p>
<p>The product infeed conveyor for vision-guided picking requires specific design features:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Consistent belt color:</strong> White or light-colored belts provide contrast against most products. Dark belts suit light-colored products.</li>
<li><strong>Controlled lighting:</strong> Overhead LED bar lights or diffuse dome lights eliminate shadows and reflections. The conveyor section under the camera often has a light enclosure to block ambient light variation.</li>
<li><strong>Encoder feedback:</strong> A belt-mounted encoder or motor encoder provides precise belt position to the robot controller. Resolution must be fine enough for the robot to track each product accurately at belt speed.</li>
<li><strong>Single-layer product:</strong> Products must arrive in a single layer, not stacked. A <a href="/conveyor-functions/product-handling/spreader/">spreader conveyor</a> or singulation conveyor upstream ensures single-layer presentation.</li>
</ul>
<p>Vision can come from a separate machine vision supplier or from the robot maker. Robot-native vision packages reduce the integration work between camera and controller.</p>
<h2 id="gripper-heading">Gripper Design</h2>
<p>The gripper (end-of-arm tool, EOAT) determines what products the robot can pick and how gently it handles them. Gripper design is the most application-specific element of a robotic case packing system. Common gripper types:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vacuum grippers:</strong> Suction cups pick products with flat or slightly curved surfaces, bags, pouches, cartons, flow-wrapped packages. Single or multi-cup configurations pick one or multiple products per cycle. Foam or bellows cups conform to irregular surfaces. Vacuum is generated by venturi (compressed air) or electric vacuum pump.</li>
<li><strong>Mechanical grippers:</strong> Fingers or jaws clamp around the product. Suited for rigid items (bottles, cans, tubes) that vacuum cups cannot grip reliably. Servo-controlled fingers adjust grip force for different product sizes.</li>
<li><strong>Fork/shovel grippers:</strong> Thin forks slide under the product (from the belt surface) and lift. Used for fragile products that cannot tolerate top-surface pressure, decorated <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/">bakery products</a>, open containers, and delicate trays.</li>
<li><strong>Multi-pick grippers:</strong> A gripper with several independent pick heads picks several products per cycle. This cuts the required robot cycle rate: a 4-pick gripper at 30 cycles per minute delivers 120 picks per minute. Common with delta robots on high-speed lines.</li>
</ul>
<p>A manual gripper change needs an operator and stops the cell. An automatic tool changer (ATC) makes the swap much faster and without tools. The robot stores multiple grippers on a rack and swaps automatically when the operator selects a new product recipe.</p>
<h2 id="conveyor-heading">Conveyor Requirements for Robotic Packing</h2>
<p>A robotic case packing cell requires three conveyor subsystems working in coordination:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Product infeed conveyor:</strong> Delivers products to the robot pick zone. Belt conveyor running at constant speed. Servo-controlled belt with encoder feedback for vision tracking. Width sized for single-file or multi-lane product presentation. <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/">Infeed system</a> upstream meters product spacing.</li>
<li><strong>Case conveyor:</strong> Positions empty cases under the robot place zone. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing conveyor</a> for stop-and-go placement, or continuous conveyor with tracking for place-on-the-fly operation. Case erector feeds empty cases from upstream.</li>
<li><strong>Case discharge conveyor:</strong> Transports loaded cases from the robot cell to case sealing and palletizing. Roller or belt conveyor with <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a> to buffer cases when the sealer stops.</li>
</ol>
<p>All three conveyors communicate through the cell PLC or robot controller. The product infeed encoder feeds the vision tracking system. The case conveyor indexes in sync with the robot cycle. The discharge conveyor receives a "case full" signal and advances the loaded case. For more on controls integration, see the <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="safety-heading">Safety Guarding &amp; Conveyor Access</h2>
<p>Robotic case packing cells require safety guarding per <a href="https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/09/09/new-ansia3-r1506-2025-american-national-standard-for-industrial-robot-safety-now-available-for-purchase/25501/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ANSI/A3 R15.06-2025</a>, the U.S. industrial robot safety standard (Part 1 covers robots, Part 2 covers robot applications and cells, Part 3 covers users). OSHA has no robot-specific standard, but the <a href="https://www.osha.gov/otm/section-4-safety-hazards/chapter-4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA Technical Manual chapter on industrial robots</a> points to R15.06 for risk assessment and safeguarding. The robot work envelope must be enclosed by safety fencing, light curtains, or area scanners. Conveyor entry and exit points through the safety perimeter use safety-rated muting sensors that allow product to pass while detecting human intrusion.</p>
<p>Collaborative robots (cobots) can operate without full safety fencing in some case packing applications, based on a risk assessment. Cobots limit speed and force when humans are nearby, and R15.06-2025 now consolidates the collaborative guidance from ISO/TS 15066. Those limits reduce throughput compared to fenced industrial robots. Cobots suit lower-volume, high-mix applications where operators frequently interact with the cell.</p>
<p>For budgeting, cell cost is driven by robot type and count, vision, gripper complexity, guarding, and conveyor requirements. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for cost factors.</p>
